; 06-var.kurt

; `var` explicitly declares a symbol as a variable: something that may stand
; for arbitrary objects, as opposed to one fixed constant (05-const.kurt).

var x, y

; Just like `const`, once a role is fixed on a level it cannot be changed
; afterwards, in either direction:
;
;     const x           ; would fail: `x` is already a variable

; Symbols starting with a dollar sign or a percent sign are *always*
; variables, no matter what -- they mark the parts of a `use` axiom that may
; be matched against anything:
;
; - `$x`, `$a`, ... stand for terms (objects), e.g. `equality.kurt` contains
;   `use $a = $a "equal-intro"` -- every object is equal to itself;
; - `%A`, `%B`, ... stand for boolean formulas, e.g. `prop.kurt` contains
;   `use %A and %B implies %A "and-elim"` -- for any formulas `%A`, `%B`.
;
; Mixing them up is a type error: `$A implies $A` is rejected, since
; `implies` needs formulas on both sides.  You'll see plenty of these once we
; `load prop` in lesson 10.
